{"name":"Neon Lagoon","hex":"#09cdcd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"neon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The chemical element (symbol Ne) with an atomic number of 10. The second of the noble gases, it is a colourless, odorless inert gas.\n A form or sample of the element.","A form or sample of the element."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That resembles a neon light; extremely bright; fluorescent"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/neon"},{"term":"lagoon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shallow body of water separated from deeper sea by a bar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lagoon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Neon","blocks":[{"text":"Neon is a chemical element; it has the symbol Ne and the atomic number 10. It is the second noble gas in the periodic table. Neon is a colorless, odorless, inert monatomic gas under standard conditions, with approximately two-thirds the density of air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Neon_discharge_tube.jpg/330px-Neon_discharge_tube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neon","wikidata":"Q654"},{"title":"Lagoon","blocks":[{"text":"A lagoon is a shallow body of water separated from a larger body of water by a narrow landform, such as a reef, a barrier island or islands, a barrier peninsula, or an isthmus. Lagoons are commonly divided into coastal lagoons and atoll lagoons. They have also been identified as occurring on mixed-sand and gravel coastlines. There is an overlap between bodies of water classified as coastal lagoons and bodies of water classified as estuaries. Lagoons are common coastal features around many parts of the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/BalosLagoonCreta.jpg/330px-BalosLagoonCreta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagoon","wikidata":"Q187223"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/NeTube.jpg/500px-NeTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/NeTube.jpg/1280px-NeTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/NeTube.jpg/500px-NeTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Neon gas-discharge lamps forming neon's element symbol","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANeTube.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG/500px-Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG/1280px-Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG/500px-Discovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The first evidence for isotopes of a stable element was provided in 1913 by experiments on neon plasma. In the bottom right corner of J. J. Thomson's photographic plate are the separate impact marks for the two isotopes neon-20 and neon-22.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiscovery_of_neon_isotopes.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg/500px-Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg/1280px-Visible_spectrum_of_neon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=